WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.817 --> 00:00:19.739
fresh evidence sounds serious," I said after reading Inspector Lestrade's telegram.

2
00:00:20.559 --> 00:00:24.241
"It's Lestrade's little boast of victory," Holmes said with a bitter smile.

3
00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:27.402
"Eat your breakfast, Watson, and then we'll go to Norwood.

4
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:31.164
I feel as if I shall need your company and your moral support today."

5
00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:34.458
In Norwood, Lestrade met us at the door of the builder's house.

6
00:00:35.458 --> 00:00:36.439
"Step this way, gentlemen.

7
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.342
I think I can convince you once and for all that it was John McFarlane who murdered Jonas

8
00:00:41.422 --> 00:00:44.785
Oldacre." He led us through the house and into a dark hall.

9
00:00:44.805 --> 00:00:50.348
"This is where McFarlane must have come out to get his hat after committing the crime," said Lestrade.

10
00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:51.789
"Look at this."

11
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:56.212
With a dramatic flourish, he struck a match and revealed a bloodstain on the wall.

12
00:00:57.813 --> 00:01:01.576
As he held the match closer to the wall, I saw that it was more than a stain.

13
00:01:02.452 --> 00:01:04.173
It was a clearly defined thumbprint.

14
00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:06.814
"Look at that with your magnifying glass, Mr.

15
00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:08.315
Holmes," said Lestrade.

16
00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:11.257
"You're aware that no two fingerprints are alike?"

17
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:16.860
"I have heard something of that kind," said Holmes, who was a well-known expert on fingerprints.

18
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:23.263
"Well then, will you please compare that print with this wax impression of McFarlane's right thumb, taken by

19
00:01:23.323 --> 00:01:24.384
my orders this morning?"

20
00:01:25.304 --> 00:01:29.687
As Lestrade held the wax print close to the bloodstain, it did not take a magnifying glass to

21
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.628
see that the two were from the same thumb.

22
00:01:32.616 --> 00:01:35.498
"That is the final piece of evidence," said Lestrade.

23
00:01:35.518 --> 00:01:39.481
"The final piece," I involuntarily echoed.

24
00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:42.483
"Final indeed," said Holmes.

25
00:01:43.464 --> 00:01:45.985
Something in his tone caught my ear, and I turned to look at him.

26
00:01:46.926 --> 00:01:50.128
He appeared to be making desperate efforts to restrain a burst of laughter.

27
00:01:50.148 --> 00:01:51.789
"Dear me!"

28
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:52.910
he said at last.

29
00:01:53.871 --> 00:01:55.632
"Well now, who would have thought it?

30
00:01:56.513 --> 00:01:58.354
How deceptive appearances may be!

31
00:01:59.234 --> 00:02:01.276
McFarlane looked like such a nice young man.

32
00:02:02.144 --> 00:02:04.866
It's a lesson for us—we should not trust our own judgment!"

33
00:02:04.886 --> 00:02:09.609
"Yes, some of us are a little too sure of ourselves," said Lestrade.

34
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:14.652
"How fortunate that this young man should press his thumb against the wall," said Holmes.

35
00:02:14.672 --> 00:02:17.975
"By the way, who made this remarkable discovery?"

36
00:02:17.995 --> 00:02:19.976
"It was the housekeeper, Mrs.

37
00:02:20.016 --> 00:02:22.618
Lexington, who drew the attention of one of my policemen to it.

38
00:02:24.219 --> 00:02:27.481
He was on guard in the bedroom last night to make sure that nothing was touched at the

39
00:02:27.521 --> 00:02:28.241
scene of the crime."

40
00:02:28.261 --> 00:02:31.764
"But why didn't the police see this mark yesterday?"

41
00:02:32.136 --> 00:02:32.717
asked Holmes.

42
00:02:32.737 --> 00:02:36.740
"We had no particular reason to make a careful examination of the hall.

43
00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:41.084
Besides, as you can see, it's not in a very obvious place."

44
00:02:42.185 --> 00:02:43.105
"No, of course not.

45
00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:46.769
I suppose there is no doubt that the mark was here yesterday."

46
00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:51.733
Lestrade looked at Holmes as if he were insane, and I confess that I was surprised at his

47
00:02:51.813 --> 00:02:53.134
rather wild observation.

48
00:02:53.154 --> 00:02:55.436
"I am a practical man, Mr.

49
00:02:55.476 --> 00:03:00.000
Holmes," the inspector said, "and when I have my evidence, I come to my conclusions.

50
00:03:01.032 --> 00:03:04.814
If you have anything more to say, you'll find me writing my report in the sitting room."

51
00:03:05.655 --> 00:03:09.097
Lestrade left us and Holmes turned to me with a gleam of merriment in his eyes.

52
00:03:09.117 --> 00:03:15.780
"There is one serious flaw in this evidence to which our friend Lestrade attaches so much importance."

53
00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:17.481
"Indeed, Holmes!

54
00:03:17.541 --> 00:03:17.982
What is it?"

55
00:03:18.002 --> 00:03:21.924
"I know that mark was not here when I examined the hall yesterday.

56
00:03:22.764 --> 00:03:26.026
And now, Watson, let's have a little stroll outside in the sunshine."

57
00:03:26.886 --> 00:03:31.594
With a confused brain but a somewhat lighter heart, I accompanied him on a walk around the garden.

58
00:03:32.414 --> 00:03:33.935
Holmes looked at each side of the house.

59
00:03:34.816 --> 00:03:38.558
He then led the way inside and went over the whole house from basement to attic.

60
00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:43.002
Most of the rooms were unfurnished, but Holmes inspected them all carefully.

61
00:03:43.862 --> 00:03:48.645
Finally he paced along the top floor, where a corridor ran outside three empty bedrooms.

62
00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:52.048
Again he was seized with a spasm of merriment.

63
00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:57.612
"It's time to let Lestrade in on some of the unique features of this case," said Holmes.

64
00:03:59.276 --> 00:04:01.698
We went downstairs and found the inspector writing his report.

65
00:04:01.718 --> 00:04:06.661
"I think you may be a little premature in writing up this case," Holmes said to Lestrade.

66
00:04:07.522 --> 00:04:09.903
Lestrade laid down his pen and looked curiously at him.

67
00:04:09.923 --> 00:04:11.584
"What do you mean?"

68
00:04:11.604 --> 00:04:15.007
"There is an important witness whom you have not seen."

69
00:04:15.027 --> 00:04:16.828
"Can you produce him?"

70
00:04:17.748 --> 00:04:20.230
"I think I can with the aid of your policemen on duty."

71
00:04:21.131 --> 00:04:23.893
Five minutes later three policemen were assembled in the sitting room.

72
00:04:24.813 --> 00:04:28.796
Holmes instructed them to bring inside two bundles of straw and two buckets of water.

73
00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:32.046
Then he led all of us up to the top floor of the house.

74
00:04:32.066 --> 00:04:37.970
"Open the window, Watson," he said, speaking in low tones, "and light the straw with one of your

75
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:44.114
matches." I did as he asked, and a coil of gray smoke swirled down the corridor while the

76
00:04:44.174 --> 00:04:45.855
dry straw crackled and flamed.

77
00:04:45.875 --> 00:04:50.118
"Now we will produce this witness, Lestrade," Holmes said softly.

78
00:04:50.138 --> 00:04:53.821
"On my command, everyone shout 'Fire!'

79
00:04:54.722 --> 00:04:59.473
Now then: one, two, three—" "Fire!"

80
00:04:59.853 --> 00:05:00.533
we all cried.

81
00:05:00.553 --> 00:05:06.778
"Fire!" Suddenly a door flew open out of what appeared to be a solid wall at the end

82
00:05:06.798 --> 00:05:07.499
of the corridor.

83
00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.622
An old man darted out of it like a rabbit darting from its burrow.

84
00:05:11.642 --> 00:05:14.465
"Yes!" said Holmes calmly.

85
00:05:14.485 --> 00:05:17.747
"Watson, throw a bucket of water on the straw.

86
00:05:18.548 --> 00:05:21.570
Lestrade, may I present your missing witness, Mr.

87
00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:22.811
Jonas Oldacre."

88
00:05:23.732 --> 00:05:25.794
Lestrade stared in amazement at the old man.

89
00:05:26.674 --> 00:05:28.436
Oldacre stood there blinking in the light.

90
00:05:29.516 --> 00:05:30.837
"What is this all about?"

91
00:05:31.278 --> 00:05:31.998
said Lestrade.

92
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:38.364
Oldacre gave an uneasy laugh, shrinking back from the furious red face of the angry inspector.

93
00:05:38.384 --> 00:05:40.266
"I have done no harm."

94
00:05:40.286 --> 00:05:42.268
"No harm?"

95
00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:43.409
said Lestrade.

96
00:05:43.429 --> 00:05:46.711
"You have done your best to get an innocent man hanged."

97
00:05:47.612 --> 00:05:49.094
The wretched creature began to whimper.

98
00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:51.256
"It was only a practical joke."

99
00:05:51.276 --> 00:05:52.837
"A joke?"

100
00:05:53.197 --> 00:05:53.998
Lestrade echoed.

101
00:05:54.018 --> 00:05:56.060
"Take him downstairs, men."

102
00:05:56.968 --> 00:06:01.752
As the policemen led Oldacre away, Holmes showed Lestrade and me the old man's hiding spot behind the

103
00:06:01.772 --> 00:06:06.275
wall. "I made up my mind that Oldacre was still in the house," Holmes said.

104
00:06:06.295 --> 00:06:11.059
"When I paced one corridor and found it six feet shorter than the one below, I knew he

105
00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:12.580
must have built himself a hiding place.

106
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.142
The only other person who knew about it was his housekeeper."

107
00:06:17.723 --> 00:06:23.568
Holmes explained that Oldacre was in financial trouble, so his intention was to deceive his creditors by disappearing.

108
00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:25.456
He wrote checks to Mr.

109
00:06:25.476 --> 00:06:28.597
Cornelius because that was the name he planned to use in his new life.

110
00:06:29.637 --> 00:06:33.799
As his final act of vengeance against the woman who refused to marry him, he wanted to make

111
00:06:33.839 --> 00:06:35.359
it appear that her son had killed him.

112
00:06:36.219 --> 00:06:40.720
When McFarlane was working on the documents, he sealed one by pressing his thumb into the wax.

113
00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:46.462
Oldacre was able to use this impression—and a few drops of his own blood—to make the thumbprint on

114
00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:46.862
the wall.

115
00:06:47.762 --> 00:06:51.243
"It's likely he had his housekeeper put the thumbprint on the wall," said Holmes.

116
00:06:51.263 --> 00:06:53.724
"But what about the remains in the fire?"

117
00:06:54.116 --> 00:06:54.596
I asked.

118
00:06:55.477 --> 00:06:58.078
"Probably just some poor animal," said Holmes.

119
00:06:59.038 --> 00:07:03.400
"At any rate, it will be Oldacre who faces a jury now, not McFarlane."

120
00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.921
Thanks to Holmes, our client would soon be free.
